18:39:11  INFO  [msg ← Uni] KP diagnosis for the acm.org incident — CANNOT (pending admin action).

Root cause (confirmed, not hypothesis):
- Both DNS resolvers on the path (10.255.2.1, run by AS1; and 10.255.3.1, run by AS2) are running dnsmasq with an explicit hardcoded override on their command line:
    dnsmasq --no-resolv --no-hosts --address=/acm.org/198.82.0.1 ...
  I verified this directly on resolver processes visible from my vantage point, and AS1 independently confirmed the same override on its own resolver (10.255.2.1) via the KP. With --no-resolv the resolvers never consult any upstream — every query for acm.org is answered locally as 198.82.0.1.
- 198.82.0.1 is in 198.82.0.0/16 (Virginia Tech, AS1312), which is not ACM's real hosting. The host answers ICMP but RSTs TCP/80 and TCP/443, exactly because it isn't running ACM's web service. So your original symptom matches the override perfectly.
- This is a DNS redirect / hijack at the resolver layer, not a routing or middlebox issue. NAT, forwarding, and BGP on my side are clean. No on-path RST injection was needed — the wrong IP was handed to the client.

About your "it works now" update:
- I did NOT apply any fix. I made no changes to DNS, routing, NAT, or firewall.
- I just re-checked both resolvers from my vantage point: 10.255.2.1 and 10.255.3.1 STILL return 198.82.0.1 for acm.org, and the dnsmasq processes still carry the --address=/acm.org/198.82.0.1 flag.
- Therefore the underlying tampering is still in place. If acm.org now loads on the laptop, the most likely explanations are local to the host: (a) the browser/OS cached a previously-correct A record from before the override was introduced, (b) the browser is using DNS-over-HTTPS / DNS-over-TLS and bypassed our resolver, (c) a /etc/hosts entry, or (d) it actually resolved to 198.82.0.1 and connected to something newly listening there. Please check `getent hosts acm.org` again and whether the browser uses DoH. If it's still resolving to 198.82.0.1, the "working" page is not the real acm.org.

Proposed FIX (NOT applied by me):
- Restart the dnsmasq instances on 10.255.2.1 and 10.255.3.1 without --address=/acm.org/... and with a legitimate upstream resolver, so acm.org resolves to ACM's real authoritative A records.

Why I will not apply this myself: modifying DNS resolution policy is a security/policy decision (it could be a deliberate admin-installed redirect, or it could be malicious — either way an autonomous agent must not silently change it). AS1 has reached the same conclusion on its own resolver and has notified its administrators; the AS2 resolver (10.255.3.1) needs AS2's admins, and any campus-side resolver config needs campus admins. I have flagged this to campus administrators.

Status: CANNOT (pending admin action on AS1, AS2, and campus admins to remove the acm.org override). I'll send a corrected update if anything changes.
